  "seoBody": "<h2>What compliance does a UK care home have to keep on top of?</h2><p>UK care home compliance is a stack of recurring checks, not a single task. A registered manager has to keep on top of mock CQC or Care Inspectorate inspections, infection prevention and control, health and safety, medication storage, safeguarding, care plan reviews, the staff training and supervision matrix, food hygiene, and accident and RIDDOR reporting. Each recurs on its own cadence.</p><p>Care home compliance software pulls those checks off paper and into one place, so the evidence is captured on the day and the group can see every home at once.</p><h2>How often each recurs</h2><p>Some areas are daily or weekly, such as medication fridge temperatures and kitchen checks. Some are monthly, such as the infection control walk and the health and safety audit. Some are quarterly or on a rolling review, such as care plans and safeguarding. Some are event-driven, such as an accident record or a RIDDOR report. Quickler holds the schedule and prompts the right person at the right time.</p><h2>How quickler runs each area</h2><p>Each area is a workflow. The person doing the check receives the prompts in their own WhatsApp and replies by text, voice note or photo. There is nothing to install and no login. Voice notes are transcribed automatically, and a photo can be attached against any finding as timestamped evidence. When a check is finished, quickler produces a shareable web report and a permanent record, and turns each issue into an action with an owner and a date. A regional quality lead sees every home's position on one dashboard.</p><h2>What quickler is not</h2><p>Quickler audits the environment, the premises and the process. It is not a care records system, it is not an eMAR, and it is not a clinical record of any resident. It does not hold care plans or medication administration rounds, and it does not perform statutory examinations such as the LOLER thorough examination or the fire and legionella risk assessments. It sits alongside your care planning and eMAR system, which stays the single source of truth for anything clinical.</p><h2>Who owns the data</h2><p>You do. Under UK GDPR the care home is the data controller and quickler is your processor, acting only on your instructions. You decide what each workflow captures. Erasure is available on request to hello@quickler.co.</p><h2>Pricing</h2><p>Pricing is only for active users: [[price:bundles]]. An active user is someone who completed at least two workflows that month, so you can add every manager, care worker and bank worker across the group and pay only for who actually files something.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What compliance does a UK care home have to keep on top of?</h3><p>Mock CQC or Care Inspectorate inspections, infection prevention and control, health and safety, medication storage, safeguarding, care plan reviews, the staff training and supervision matrix, food hygiene, and accident and RIDDOR reporting.</p><h3>Is quickler a care records or eMAR system?</h3><p>No. Quickler is not a care records, eMAR or clinical system. It is the audit and evidence layer that captures your checks and inspections, and it sits alongside your care records system rather than replacing it.</p><h3>Who owns the data?</h3><p>You do. Under UK GDPR the care home is the data controller and quickler is your processor, acting on your instructions.</p>",
